  [PASS] V6 [CURRENT kSZ CANNOT TEST THIS, AND NOT BY A LITTLE] the framework's predicted fractional v_12 shift is divided by the total fractional amplitude error of a pairwise kSZ measurement, statistical and optical-depth errors combined in quadrature, over every combination of the published detection significances and the published tau uncertainties; the threshold for the claim 'current kSZ can see it' is that the best entry reach 3 sigma
           (the best entry in the table reaches 0.30 sigma, and that entry already assumes INFINITE statistics and the most optimistic 10% optical depth. With the actual best pairwise measurement (5.4 sigma, 19% statistical) and a 15% tau the significance is 0.09 sigma. THIS IS THE FINDING: the kSZ angle is not a test of this framework today, and the obstacle is the optical depth, not the photon noise)

V7 -- what it would take: the required tau precision and the required statistics
    [canonical] shift 2.12%: a 2 sigma statement needs sigma_tau/tau < 1.06% (with perfect statistics) AND an overall kSZ detection at 94 sigma (with perfect tau) -- naively 306x the object count of the 5.4 sigma measurement, since SNR scales as sqrt(N_pairs)
    [canonical] shift 2.12%: a 3 sigma statement needs sigma_tau/tau < 0.71% (with perfect statistics) AND an overall kSZ detection at 142 sigma (with perfect tau) -- naively 689x the object count of the 5.4 sigma measurement, since SNR scales as sqrt(N_pairs)
    [alt] shift 2.97%: a 2 sigma statement needs sigma_tau/tau < 1.48% (with perfect statistics) AND an overall kSZ detection at 67 sigma (with perfect tau) -- naively 156x the object count of the 5.4 sigma measurement, since SNR scales as sqrt(N_pairs)
    [alt] shift 2.97%: a 3 sigma statement needs sigma_tau/tau < 0.99% (with perfect statistics) AND an overall kSZ detection at 101 sigma (with perfect tau) -- naively 350x the object count of the 5.4 sigma measurement, since SNR scales as sqrt(N_pairs)
    the binding requirement: sigma_tau/tau < 0.71%, against an optimistic present-day 10% -- a factor 14 in the optical depth alone
    [canonical] the one partial handle -- the redshift DIFFERENTIAL, which a z-independent tau error cancels from: the boost is +2.7% at z = 0.3 against +1.1% at z = 1.0, a differential of +1.63% -- SMALLER than the signal it was meant to rescue
    [alt] the one partial handle -- the redshift DIFFERENTIAL, which a z-independent tau error cancels from: the boost is +3.8% at z = 0.3 against +1.6% at z = 1.0, a differential of +2.15% -- SMALLER than the signal it was meant to rescue
  [PASS] V7 [THE REQUIREMENT IS STATED, AND IT IS A FACTOR OF SEVERAL IN A SYSTEMATIC NOBODY CONTROLS] the optical-depth precision needed for a 3 sigma statement about the framework's v_12 shift is solved for and compared with the most optimistic optical-depth precision the published literature claims; a factor above 3 means the gap is not closable by better CMB maps
           (sigma_tau/tau < 0.71% is required against 10% claimed at best, a factor 14. The redshift differential, the only feature of the prediction a constant tau error cannot absorb, is 2.15% -- smaller than the amplitude it would replace, so it is not a rescue. What WOULD change the verdict is an independent measurement of the electron column: fast-radio-burst dispersion measures cross-correlated with the same tracers (the Macquart relation measures the same integral of n_e that tau does, with completely different systematics), or a jointly calibrated gas profile from tSZ + X-ray + FRB, feeding a CMB-S4-era kSZ measurement. The photon-noise side is the easy half)

V8 -- the direction of the effect, and how it sits against the S_8 tension
    the framework RAISES the growth rate, so infall is faster and |v_12| is LARGER: +2.12% (canonical) / +2.97% (alt)
    the same kernel raises S_8 to 0.843 / 0.847 against Planck's 0.834, while KiDS-1000 measures 0.766 +- 0.020 (LITERATURE: Asgari+21)
    direct lensing pulls at -3.4 sigma relative to Planck -- i.e. DOWNWARD, toward less growth; the kernel pulls UPWARD. The two probes pull in OPPOSITE directions
    but L197's kicks cancel the kernel at 8 h^-1 Mpc, so the FULL framework predicts S_8 ~ LCDM (L197: 0.843/0.845 vs LCDM 0.842) while leaving v_12 raised at 10-100 h^-1 Mpc (V5)
  [PASS] V8 [kSZ AND DIRECT LENSING PULL IN OPPOSITE DIRECTIONS, AND THE FRAMEWORK'S OWN SIGNATURE IS A SCALE SPLIT] the sign of the predicted v_12 shift is multiplied by the sign of the weak-lensing pull relative to Planck; a product below zero means a kSZ confirmation of raised large-scale growth would sit against, not with, the low-S_8 direct measurements
           (the v_12 shift is +2.12%/+2.97% (UPWARD) while KiDS-1000 sits -3.4 sigma below Planck (DOWNWARD): product -1. This is a genuinely useful consistency statement and it cuts both ways. The framework does NOT resolve the S_8 tension -- it inherits Planck's side of it (G020, glm53 lane) -- and a future high-precision kSZ that confirmed raised 10-100 Mpc growth while lensing stayed low would put the framework and LCDM under the SAME strain, since both would then need scale-dependent growth. The framework's distinctive claim is not the raise by itself but the SPLIT: LCDM-like at 8 h^-1 Mpc (kernel cancelled by kicks) and raised at 10-100 h^-1 Mpc (kernel alone). That split is the thing to preregister if the tau problem is ever solved. NOTE the honest cost: the split depends on the clock-frame kicks, which L197 itself records as carrying a Lyman-alpha forest problem 164x over bound)

